# xhci98.sys import allowlist (x86) - module/symbol pairs the driver may
# import. scripts\import-gate\check-imports.ps1 enforces it after every link.
#
# SINCE 2026-10-02 xhci98.sys IS THE SUCCESSOR HOST CONTROLLER DRIVER (design
# record 13): the usbport miniport left the tree, and with it this file's
# usbport rows, its Option A arguments and its "allocate no pool" policy. The
# miniport's last allowlist is on branch 1.2.0.0.
#
# An unresolved import stops the driver before DriverEntry on both targets,
# with no call-site diagnostic (docs\usb-xhci-info\win98-wdm.md "Imports are
# a silent load-time gate"). This file is the committed decision about which
# pairs may appear in the linked binary.
#
# Pairs, not names. The PE import descriptor names the providing module, so
# NTOSKRNL.EXE!DbgPrint and HAL.DLL!DbgPrint are different imports and only one
# of them resolves. Module names are matched case-insensitively; symbol names
# exactly.
#
# Format, one entry per line, whitespace separated:
#
#   MODULE!SYMBOL   FLAVORS   REQUIREMENT   evidence notes to end of line
#
#   FLAVORS      release | debug | qemu | all - which build flavor may import it
#                ("both" is retired and refused: there are three flavours)
#   REQUIREMENT  required | optional
#
# Evidence tags in the last column, strongest first. check-imports.ps1
# re-derives all three when the extracted target files are present:
#
#   w2k-export         in the export table of every Windows 2000 SP4 kernel
#                      and HAL image (win2k-baselines.expected).
#   win98-precedent    an authenticated Windows 98 SE or NUSB binary
#                      (win98-evidence.list) imports the same pair.
#   ntkern-name        the name is a NUL-delimited string in Windows 98 SE's
#                      ntkern.vxd. Positive evidence only, and module-blind:
#                      task 25.3 found 19 names stock 98 SE drivers import that
#                      are absent from it (design record 13 section 7).
#
# Every row is added with its Windows 98 evidence: a w2k-export tick alone does
# not admit a pair. The authoritative Windows 98 check is still the load itself.
#
# Three rules are the gate's rather than this file's:
#
#   - No USBPORT.SYS pair is allowed at all. The HCD replaces usbport.sys
#     (roadmap-hcd.md, "The shape of the successor"), and a stock Windows 98 SE
#     has none to resolve against.
#   - An image that imports nothing at all passes only while it carries the
#     scaffold marker XHCI98_SCAFFOLD_DO_NOT_STAGE (task 25.8). Once 26-A.1
#     removes the marker, an empty import table is a failure.
#   - Pool is allowed by rows here, with the rule naming the sites that may
#     allocate (design record 13 section 7; the owner's decision of 2026-10-02,
#     roadmap-hcd.md decisions table, "Pool and DMA"). Until 26-A.1 adds the
#     rows, the pool family is denied below so an allocation cannot arrive
#     ahead of its evidence.

[imports]

# Task 25.8's scaffold imports nothing. Phase 26 task 26-A.1 grows this
# section to what the FDO of 26-A.2 imports, from task 25.3's evidence table
# (design record 13 section 7 and Appendix A).

[deny]

# Where the reason is a fact about a target - absent on Windows 98, or
# blocking the load on Windows 2000 - and so binds any binary on those
# targets. The miniport's denials argued from Option A ("usbport owns it") are
# gone with it.

ExAllocatePool              Not the HCD's spelling: task 25.3 found no Windows 98 SE or NUSB binary importing the untagged name, while ExAllocatePoolWithTag has dozens of stock 98 SE importers (design record 13 section 7). The HCD allocates with ExAllocatePoolWithTag and frees with ExFreePool.
ExAllocatePoolWithTag       The HCD's allocation entry point once 26-A.1 adds its row (task 25.3: stock Windows 98 SE drivers import it from NTOSKRNL.EXE). Denied until then, so an allocation cannot arrive ahead of the row and the site rule that come with it.
ExFreePool                  The HCD's free entry point once 26-A.1 adds its row (task 25.3: stock Windows 98 SE drivers import it). Denied until then, with ExAllocatePoolWithTag.
ExFreePoolWithTag           No Windows 98 evidence at all (task 25.3: no stock 98 SE driver imports it, and NUSB 3.6's wdmstub.sys appears to supply it), so it stays denied after the pool rows arrive. Free with ExFreePool.
MmGetSystemRoutineAddress   Missing on Win98 (Oney p.438). There is no runtime symbol resolution on that target - every import decision is static and made here.
KeNumberProcessors          Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.441).
IoAllocateWorkItem          Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.375); task 25.3 found it among the names NUSB 3.6's wdmstub.sys supplies. ExQueueWorkItem has stock 98 SE precedent.
IoQueueWorkItem             Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.375).
IoFreeWorkItem              Not exported on Win98 (Oney p.375).
IoAcquireRemoveLock         The IO_REMOVE_LOCK family is missing on Win98 SE (Oney p.191). Keep an outstanding-I/O count instead.
IoReleaseRemoveLock         IO_REMOVE_LOCK family - missing on Win98 SE (Oney p.191).
IoReleaseRemoveLockAndWait  IO_REMOVE_LOCK family - missing on Win98 SE (Oney p.191).
MmMapLockedPagesSpecifyCache  Missing on Win98; MmGetSystemAddressForMdlSafe expands to it (Oney p.225). Task 25.7's transfer route needs no system mapping of a URB buffer.
MmGetPhysicalAddress        No Windows 98 binary precedent in any of the 86 scanned (task 25.3), and it would bypass the HAL's map registers; transfer buffers go through the DMA adapter's MapTransfer (design record 13 section 11).
HalGetAdapter               No Windows 98 binary precedent (task 25.3). The DMA adapter comes from IoGetDmaAdapter, as Windows 98 SE's own uhcd.sys and openhci.sys get theirs, and the two routes are never mixed (design record 13 section 11).
HalAllocateCommonBuffer     No Windows 98 binary precedent (task 25.3). Under ntddk.h a call by this name is a HAL import; call the adapter's DMA_OPERATIONS slot instead.
HalFreeCommonBuffer         Same as HalAllocateCommonBuffer.
PsGetVersion                Missing on Win98 (Oney p.437). Use IoIsWdmVersionAvailable.
KeReadStateEvent            Missing on Win98 (Oney p.95).
IoReportTargetDeviceChangeAsynchronous  Not exported on Win98 - a load-blocking import, not a runtime failure (Oney p.191).
PoRegisterDeviceNotify      Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoCancelDeviceNotify        Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoRegisterSystemState       Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoSetSystemState            Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
PoUnregisterSystemState     Not implemented on Win98 (Oney p.254).
HalTranslateBusAddress      Only partially available on Win98 (Oney p.441). Use the translated resources PnP supplies in IRP_MN_START_DEVICE.
KeAcquireInterruptSpinLock  Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000 (docs\usb-xhci-info\win98-wdm.md). Use KeSynchronizeExecution, which the HCD can now call because it owns its interrupt object.
KeReleaseInterruptSpinLock  Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000.
IoForwardIrpSynchronously   Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000. Use IoCallDriver plus a completion routine that signals a KEVENT.
IoSetCompletionRoutineEx    Windows XP and later - blocks the load on Win2000.
IoCsqInitialize             Cancel-safe queues arrived with the XP DDK and are not in the Win2K DDK's libraries.
IoCsqInsertIrp              Cancel-safe queue family - not in the Win2K DDK.
IoCsqRemoveNextIrp          Cancel-safe queue family - not in the Win2K DDK.
